{"kind":"run-header","instance":"newman-mu","seed":"terms-smoke-baseline","generator":"enum","mock":false,"harnessSha":"78dbfd3e179716ad67ba2380afffab3d70f0b98ea96dddff7000bb405d8fc56f","config":{"batchSize":32,"decades":[2],"rejectAuditRate":0.05,"maxCandidates":60},"controls":{"scoreBattery":{"plantedChecked":5,"knownBadScore":-1,"pass":true},"recall":{"planted":5,"pass":true},"certifierNegative":{"pass":true}},"statement":"For a Newman polynomial f(z) = sum_{a in A} z^a with A a set of n non-negative integers (coefficients 0/1), let M(A) = min over |z|=1 of |f(z)|. A HIT is an n-term A whose certified lower bound on M(A)^2 strictly exceeds bar(n)^2, where bar(n) is the largest certified M over all anchors with FEWER than n terms — i.e. a Newman polynomial that beats everything achievable with fewer terms. The certificate is an exact enclosure of the global minimum of |f|^2 = n + 2*sum_{i<j} cos((a_j-a_i)theta), reduced to a global minimum of an integer-coefficient polynomial on [-1,1] via cos(d*theta) = T_d(cos theta) and certified by Sturm isolation plus interval-Newton refinement with exact rational value enclosures (instruments/trigmin). bar(n) is COMPUTED from pinned witness exponent sets at load time, never transcribed. A separate TRIPWIRE flags any certified M >= 2 carried by at most 18 terms, which would improve the certified record that the least n with mu(n) >= 2 is at most 19; a tripwire is a flag for the owner and is never a result.","statementSha":"d610cd1359d7a91db0088b0ceb9f3248a099b31fa8b4174942a4070b85e286d6","chain":"9eeca30c4e41942eef48709015044f573d6e47c45e5bd8622816fd556051b03c"}
